From 66541c3eab770d0402dcb41fc5777bbdd6df0b95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Eduardo=20Garc=C3=ADa-M=C3=A1dico=20Portabella?=
 <woodyst@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 19:08:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] loc: add the constellation control messages, and an
 adapter for the query

These three messages exist in the modem's LOC service but are published
nowhere. Their layout was recovered from the QMI IDL table that the vendor
location library carries (loc_qmi_idl_service_object_v02), and every field
was cross-checked against the code that builds each request:

  0x0088  Set GNSS Constellation Report Config
  0x00B8  Set Constellation Control
  0x00BF  Get Constellation Control

The decoding is not a plausible reading. A decoder written against that
table recomputes the maximum length of every one of the 466 messages that
have a type of their own, and matches the value the library itself stores,
in all 466; the C struct sizes fall out on their own; the vendor code that
fills message 0x0088 writes at offsets 0, 8, 16 and 24, exactly where the
decoding says the fields are; and a real modem answers the query with a
49-byte payload, which is the maximum the decoding predicts.

Note the asymmetry, which is why the adapter is worth having: the setters
take opaque 64-bit constellation bitmasks, while the getter reports one TLV
per constellation. The bit order of those masks is NOT the same in every
message -- the one taken by Set Constellation Control is written straight
from Android's GnssSvTypesMask, while the multi-band conversion uses a
different order -- so they are left as plain guint64 rather than given a
named flags type that would look authoritative without being so.

The per-constellation TLVs of the getter have no such ambiguity, and
qmi_indication_loc_get_constellation_control_output_get_constellations()
collapses them into one enabled/disabled pair, which is the shape a
location manager actually wants.

The state values are an unpublished enumeration. The vendor stack splits
them into two groups, up to 2 and 100 to 103, but does not say which is
which; that was settled on hardware. A device tracking GPS and GLONASS
reports 0 for GPS, 1 for the rest and 100 for NavIC, so the low group is
the one that is on -- and NavIC being the odd one out fits, being regional.

A constellation the modem did not report lands in neither set, and so does
one whose state value is not recognised: absent is not the same as
disabled, and neither is guessed at.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
